diff --git a/python/ttcap/how-to-capture-welland-pi.md b/python/ttcap/how-to-capture-welland-pi.md
index 76fc991..0d51d3a 100644
--- a/python/ttcap/how-to-capture-welland-pi.md
+++ b/python/ttcap/how-to-capture-welland-pi.md
@@ -44,9 +44,13 @@ The fpgas.online bridge (`fpgas-tt`) holds the board's serial port, `/dev/ttboar
 
 Steps 3 and 6 are those of [How to stop the fpgas-tt daemon and start it again](https://docs.fpgas.online/en/latest/boards/tt-fpga/setup/stop-daemon.html). Step 5 comes before step 6 because the bridge checks the board's state when it starts. On a demo board with an ASIC breakout, starting the bridge with the capture's project still enabled left it reporting `left:` and that project ([result](https://github.com/mithro/tt-vga-capture/tree/main/docs/results/2026-11-24-direct-serial-fpga4)). That report is in `safe_start.state` in the bridge's health output ([result](https://github.com/mithro/tt-vga-capture/tree/main/docs/results/2026-11-26-docs-bench-ttcap)). `ttcap demo --board fpga-4` run on this Pi picks `serial:/dev/ttboard` itself, and says to stop the bridge first.
 
+Step 4 captures the design already enabled. On an idle board that is the idle display, which has no VGA frames, so `ttcap png` prints `frames=0` for it. For pictures, enable a VGA design before step 3, while `/health` shows `"clients": 0`: `curl -s -X POST localhost:8765/designs/tt_um_vga_pattern/enable` did so on fpga-1 (bench run of PR #56, 2026-11-27). Render the capture on a workstation, as [How to run ttcap on a Raspberry Pi](how-to-run-on-raspberry-pi.md) explains.
+
 ## Check
 
-Step 4 ends with `wrote bench.vgacap` and a stats line. It shows `overruns=0 dropped=0 rxstall=0` when no sample was lost. 500 kHz was clean in one 4-second run on fpga-1's USB serial, and 750 kHz overran on fpga-4 ([result](https://github.com/mithro/tt-vga-capture/tree/main/docs/results/2026-11-26-docs-bench-ttcap)). How long 500 kHz stays clean is open ([#52](https://github.com/mithro/vgacap/issues/52)). After step 6, `systemctl is-active fpgas-tt` prints `active`, and `sudo fuser /dev/ttboard` shows the bridge's process again.
+Step 4 prints a stats line, which shows `overruns=0 dropped=0 rxstall=0` when no sample was lost, and ends with `wrote bench.vgacap`. 500 kHz was clean in one 4-second run on fpga-1's USB serial, and 750 kHz overran on fpga-4 ([result](https://github.com/mithro/tt-vga-capture/tree/main/docs/results/2026-11-26-docs-bench-ttcap)). How long 500 kHz stays clean is open ([#52](https://github.com/mithro/vgacap/issues/52)). After step 6, `systemctl is-active fpgas-tt` prints `active`, and `sudo fuser /dev/ttboard` shows the bridge's process again.
+
+On fpga-1, step 7 reached `"state": "loaded"` after 72 s. At about 67 s, one poll showed `"clients": 1`, and the next showed `"clients": 0` with `loaded` (bench run of PR #56, 2026-11-27). Keep polling through it.
 
